# Functionalization of Metal-Organic Frameworks for Photoactive Materials

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are intriguing platforms with multiple functionalities. Additional functionalization of MOFs generates novel materials for various applications. Here, three main topics are examined regarding the functionalization of MOFs for use as photoactive materials. The first is chemical approaches for postsynthetic modification of the metal clusters and organic linkers in MOFs; that is, sites on pore surfaces and chemical trapping of photoactive moieties within the pores, which create materials with chemical functionalities for water splitting and CO2 reduction by light. The second topic focuses on the functionalization of MOFs for photochemical response and the versatile applications of such materials. State-of-the-art research on functionalizing MOFs through photochemical reactions on the pore surface and within the pores as guests is also summarized. The third topic introduces the functionalization of MOFs for photofunctional materials, including photoluminescent tuning and integration, photoluminescent LED devices and barcodes, and photophysical applications for chemical sensing. Finally, conclusions and perspectives on the fields are given.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are intriguing platforms with multi­ple functionalities. Additional functionalization of MOFs generates novel materials for various applications. Here, three main topics are examined regarding the functionalization of MOFs for use as photoactive materials. The first is chemical approaches for postsynthetic modification of the metal clusters and organic linkers in MOFs; that is, sites on pore surfaces and chemical trapping of photoactive moieties within the pores, which create materials with chemical functionalities for water splitting and $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ reduction by light. The second topic focuses on the functionalization of MOFs for photochemical response and the versatile applications of such materials. State-of-the-art research on functionalizing MOFs through photochemical reactions on the pore surface and within the pores as guests is also summarized. The third topic introduces the functionalization of MOFs for photofunctional materials, including photoluminescent tuning and integration, photoluminescent LED devices and barcodes, and photophysical applications for chemical sensing. Finally, conclusions and perspectives on the fields are given.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), also called “porous coordination polymers” (PCPs), are an emerging class of crystalline porous materials formed by the self-assembly of polydentate organic bridging ligands, which act as struts, and metal connecting points, which act as nodes.[1] The special nanosized cavities and/or open channels in MOFs provide structural diversity and outstanding tenability. The modular nature (via the combination of inorganic and organic components) of these materials makes them ideally suited for chemical manipulations aimed at fine tuning the structure and function. In recent years, MOFs have attracted attention due to their potential applications for gas storage/separation/release,[2–4] catalysis,[5] molecular sensing,[6] and drug delivery[7] among others.[8]
MOFs are unique biomimetic crystal lattices that can integrate any number of multicomponent functionalities, and they provide a versatile platform for covalently combining the photosensitizer and catalyst into a two-component artificial photo­synthesis system. The ordered porosity in MOFs allows functional species to be encapsulated, which further enhances their tunability for photocatalytic applications. In addition, the functionalization of MOFs by photochemical reactions can occur under mild conditions and be used to transform the structure and functionalities of MOFs without unwanted side reactions.[9–11] These transformations can be in situ, spatially precise and controlled by light. Therefore, photochemical reactions are regarded as a pertinent method for subtle functionalization of MOFs without collapsing the framework. Since the single crystallinity of the MOFs remains intact during the transformation process, the MOF intermediates and final products can be unambiguously characterized via X-ray crystallography. Inspired by natural photo­synthesis and photochemical reactions and utilizing their virtues, MOFs have been developed as advanced materials for various applications, such as generating highly reactive species and switching gas adsorption properties. In addition, because luminescent components, such as inorganic metal nodes, organic ligands, and metal–organic charge transfer compounds, can be encapsulated within MOFs as guest species, luminescent MOFs are rapidly emerging as photoactive materials with a wide variety of applications. Currently, studies on luminescent MOFs have included tuning optical properties for applications in LEDs and barcodes and developing chemical sensors for environmental monitoring, occupational safety, food quality control, and chemical threat detection.
Here, three main topics on functionalized MOFs for photoactive materials, involving both photochemical and photophysical aspects are summarized. The first two topics include photochemical applications, the introduction of different chemical functional groups onto MOFs for artificial photosynthesis and functionalization of MOFs by photochemical reactions for photochemical responses and their applications. The final topic is photophysical applications of functionalized MOFs, especially for photoluminescent materials and chemical sensing. These photoactive materials have great potential applications in both photochemical and photophysical fields because of their versatile crystal structures and function integration.
## 2. Functionalization of MOFs for Photocatalytic Solar Fuel Production
Solar fuel production involves the direct conversion of sunlight into highly energetic chemicals using an artificial photocatalyst.[12–16] In recent years, MOFs have shown great potential as promising artificial photocatalysts. Unlike other inorganic materials, MOFs provide an ideal platform to integrate both the metal nodes (i.e., catalytically active moieties) and organic linkers (i.e., photosensitizer[17]) and are amenable to functionalization. Moreover, the exceptionally high surface area and porosity of MOFs allow for further encapsulation of catalytic or light-absorbing guest species. In this section, we focus on the molecular modification of MOFs for photocatalytic water splitting and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reactions, and the modifications are classified by the different functionalized components of the MOFs. MOF-based photocatalytic organic transformations[18,19] and MOF-based composites[20,21] have been previously reviewed and are not included in this section.
## 2.1. Photocatalytic Hydrogen Production
## 2.1.1. Functionalization of Metal Clusters for Hydrogen Production
In 2009, Kataoka et al. reported the first example of a porous MOF as a hydrogen production photocatalyst, and the MOF had a layered square grid topology with $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ paddlewheels as the nodes and bdc (bdc $^{=1,4}$ -benzenedicarboxylate) as the struts.[22] In the presence of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ as the photosensitizer, $\mathbf{\Psi}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethyl$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridinium as the electron relay, and ethylenediamine tetraacetic acid (EDTA) as the electron donor, the diruthenium cores in the framework served as catalytic sites for hydrogen production with a quantum yield of $4.82\%$ at $420\mathrm{nm}$ . One year later, Silva et al. studied the photocatalytic hydrogen production over 3D, porous ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ and $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (UiO $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ University of Oslo) MOFs upon UV irradiation at $\lambda>300~\mathrm{nm}$ .[23] Since $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ oxo-clusters have a more positive conduction band potential than their ${\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}}$ counterpart, Horiuchi et al. reported hydrogen production using MIL-125(Ti)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda<$ $500\mathrm{nm}_{\cdot}$ ).[24] In addition, a postsynthetic metal exchange approach was employed to prepare ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{4+}{\mathrm{.}}$ -substituted $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and over $50\%$ of the ${\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}}$ ions in the $\mathrm{zr}$ oxo-clusters was replaced by the smaller $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ ions.[25] The resultant MOF, UiO-66- $(\mathrm{Zr/Ti}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , produced ${\approx}1.5$ times more $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ than the pristine $\mathrm{JiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ after $^{\mathrm{~9~h~}}$ of visible-light irradiation, and this was attributed to the improved interfacial charge transfer of $\mathrm{(Ti/Zr)_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ . Feng et al. prepared a layered MOF, $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{PymS})_{4}$ $(\mathrm{PymSH}~=$ pyrimidine-2-thiol), with a binuclear $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ node, which is structurally similar to the active site in [NiFe]-hydrogenase.[26] Using fluorescein as the photosensitizer and trimethylamine (TEA) as the electron donor, the photocatalytic hydrogen production turnover frequency reached $10.6\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ upon white-light LED illumination.
