## 2.2. Photocatalytic Water Oxidation
Water oxidation, the oxidative water splitting half reaction, is kinetically sluggish and requires a four-electron transfer process and the removal of four protons from water molecules.
## 2.3. Photocatalytic Reduction of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is widely recognized as a major contributor to global warming, and one of the best mitigation strategies is to utilize $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as a renewable $\mathrm{C}_{1}$ feedstock for commercially relevant chemicals. Recently, extensive studies have focused on MOFs as ideal materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and conversion due to their high surface area and tunability for chemical functionalization.[49–54] Herein, we focus on heterogeneous reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ using a light source.
## 2.3.1. Functionalization of Metal Clusters for $C O_{2}$ Reduction
Fu et al. realized the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into formate over MIL-125(Ti)- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ under visible-light irradiation, and the reduction was performed in acetonitrile using TEOA as the sacrificial electron donor.[55] The isotopic $^{13}{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ studies confirmed that the formate products originated from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ despite the low catalytic activity. U $\mathrm{iO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was used as an example MOF to investigate the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction mechanism.[56] The photoluminescence and ESR studies indicated a photoinduced electron transfer from the excited ligand to the $\mathrm{zr}$ nodes to form $\mathrm{Zr^{III}}$ as the active site. A series of Fecontaining MOFs (MIL-101(Fe), MIL-53(Fe), and MIL-88B(Fe)) were investigated for the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into formate under visible-light irradiation, and direct excitation of the Fe oxo-clusters was observed via the ESR signals.[57] Meanwhile, amino-functionalization of the MOF provided a second excitation pathway for photoinduced electron transfer from the amino-antenna to the Fe centers. Wang et al. reported that the cobalt-containing zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-9(Co)) is a highly robust and recyclable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction photocatalyst in the presence of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ as the photosensitizer and TEOA as the sacrificial agent.[58] Under visible-light irradiation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was split into CO with a TON reaching $\scriptstyle\approx450$ during a $2.5\mathrm{~h~}$ reaction, and the porosity of both the ZIF-9(Co) and benzimid­ azolate motifs affected the photocatalytic conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
