Photochemistry with MOFs is particularly intriguing for both basic science and practical applications due to the combination of advantages. MOFs have been comprehensively investigated as photocatalysts, and excellent reviews have been written on photocatalytic transformations[69–71] and decontamination of wastewater.[72] The second section here has focused on photocatalytic water splitting and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and thus, they are excluded from this section. The pivotal nodes of MOFs are constructed by coordination bonds; therefore, MOF frameworks cannot tolerate harsh chemical conditions, such as a strong base or acid or moisture.[73] Because of this, MOFs have been represented as soft and dynamic balance systems.[74] Functionalization of MOFs by chemical reactions must be carefully implemented to maintain the porous and crystalline structures. A photon is a unique reaction reagent
## 3.1. Functionalization of MOFs by Photochemical Reactions on Organic Ligands
## 3.1.1. Functionalization of MOFs by Photoactivation Reactions
$\ln{2010}$ , Kitagawa’s group reported using a photochemical reaction to activate functional groups on MOF organic ligands.[76] They demonstrated a new strategy to create MOF materials decorated with highly reactive, ‘bare’ nitrenes on the pore surface. They synthesized single crystals of a MOF $\left(\mathrm{CID-N}_{3}\right)$ composed of aryl azide ligands $(\mathrm{N}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{a})$ , which exhibited a 1D channel with aryl azides regularly exposed on the channel surface. Photoirradiation on dried $\mathrm{CID-N}_{3}$ in situ converted the stable azide groups into bare triplet nitrenes (Figure 4), and photoirradiation on dried CID- ${\bf N}_{3}$ in an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ atmosphere $(80~\mathrm{\kPa})$ resulted in the generation of nitro- and nitrosofunctionalized MOFs through nitrene intermediates in the porous solid state. An unexpected photoreaction was recently reported by Stock and co-workers.[77] Upon photoirradiation of a nitroisophthalic acid-based MOF containing guest molecules, a stable radical was created, which was attributed to the formation of an anionic nitroso radical from the organic nitro ligand. Photoactivation is also a useful synthetic approach for postsynthetic modification (PSM), which was demonstrated by Tanabe et al. in 2010.[78] They photoinduced the removal of protecting groups from the organic ligands on a MOF lattice in a single-crystal-to-single-crystal (SCSC) transformation. First, photolabile but thermally stable protecting groups were incorporated into organic ligands to prepare the MOF materials. Then, photoirradiation was used to cleave the $\textit{o}$ -nitrobenzyl protecting groups and prepared rare MOFs with uncoordinated hydroxyl groups.[79] Using a combination of postsynthetic deprotection (PSD) and PSM methods, a strategy to synthesize multi-functionalized isoreticular MOFs (IRMOFs) was developed.[80] Photoactivation of MOFs to release bioactive compounds, such as NO and CO gases, is promising for biological applications. To generate biomolecular NO, Furukawa and co-workers used nitroaromatic compounds (2-nitroimidazole and 5-methyl-4-nitroimidazole) to synthesize ZIFs.[81] The nitro-to-nitrite rearrangement occurred via photoactivation, and the subsequent bond cleavage led to the production and release of NO in the porous coordination polymers. They also developed a series of MOFs functionalized with $N\mathrm{.}$ -nitrosamine groups by the PSM method that can be used as photoactive NO donors.[82] A similar protocol was applied for the controllable release of CO in cellular substrates using a photoactive manganese carbonyl complex instead of $N$ -nitrosamine.[83] In general, photoinduced isomerizations are also considered photoreactions that can facilely switch functionalities. In 2011, Bernt et al. reported a ZIF that was partially immobilized with phenylazo groups.[84] Upon photoirradiation at different wavelengths, the cis and trans forms are switchable and partially reversible. In 2012, they used a $\mathrm{PSM}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to synthesize azobenzene derivative-functionalized MOFs.[85] The cis/trans isomerization of these MOFs was partially reversible with photoirradiation, which led to small variations in the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption behavior. Zhou et al. reported a better system using an azo group-functionalized terephthalate as the ligand to synthesize isostructural MOF-5, which shows reversible $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behavior upon UV irradiation or heat treatment.[86] Another protocol was demonstrated by Lyndon et al., and they synthesized a MOF composed of azo pillar ligands in contrast to the incorporation of pendant azo groups.[87] Upon photoirradiation, the MOF exhibited dynamic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption switching that was triggered by slight structural conformation changes. Moreover, this work indicated that photoenergy can be directly converted to mechanical energy to facilitate the release of trapped gases.
## 3.1.2. Functionalization of MOFs by Photocycloaddition Reactions
Photocycloaddition plays a central role in photochemistry, and thus, it is frequently used for the functionalization of MOFs.
