dition in MOFs was advanced by several groups. Park et al. reported the conversion of a twofold, interpenetrated, nonporous MOF based on bpeb ligands to a non-interpenetrated, 3D network with a new topology by a $[2\ +\ 2]$ photocycloaddition reaction in an SCSC manner.[95] Using bpeb as a ligand, a structural transformation from a sixfold, interpenetrated, diamondoid network to a non-interpenetrated structure was realized in an SCSC manner, and interestingly, this transformation process was reversible with heating.[96] Yang et al. reported a $[2+2]$ photodimerization of guest molecules in channels with the host ligands on a MOF, and a synergistic relationship among the three reaction (quasi) equilibria and three types of photochemical reactions (poly­ merization, dimerization, and pedal-like isomerization) was demonstrated.[97] In 2015, Park et al. reported a sophisticated MOF transformation by a photocycloaddition reaction in an SCSC manner.[98] In particular, a conformational change in the bpeb ligand pulled the olefin bonds in the bpeb ligands closer and facilitated the polymerization reaction to form a syndiotactic polymer (Figure 5). In 2016, Foo et al. reported a 1,2-di(4- pyridyl)ethylene (dpe)-based MOF that exhibited 0D pores and a discriminatory adsorbate gate effect.[99] This feature led to the selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , but the selectivity was eliminated by a $[2+2]$ photochemical cycloaddition of dpe ligands to the MOF. Hazra et al. reported the facile modulation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity and magnetic properties in a bimodal MOF by photochemical $[2~+~2]$ cycloaddition reactions between the dpe ligands and guest molecules.[100]
## 3.1.3. Functionalization of MOFs by Photochromic Reactions
Photochromic molecules can switch between the open and closed forms by photons, especially dithienylethenes (DTEs). Incorporation of DTEs into MOFs will render photoresponsive MOF materials with novel fluorescence and sorption properties. In 2013, Walton et al. reported that the inclusion of DTE guests within the MOF produced a photoresponsive MOF.[101] The photochromic guests aligned in the pores and had excellent thermal stability. Additionally, a DTE-based MOF was synthesized, but the resulting photochromism of the MOF was irreversible.[102] This phenomenon was illustrated by computational studies based on a slightly modified model and attributed to the large interconversion energetic barrier.[103] This problem was finally solved by immobilizing DTE-based ligands within an extended porphyrin metal coordination framework.[104] The emission of this MOF could be reversibly switched by activating the ET pathways through photoisomerization of the DTE-based ligands. In 2014, Luo et al. reported the synthesis of a MOF using a DTE-based ligand.[105] The photochromic reaction was reversible with photoirradiation and resulted in subtle movement of the framework, which led to the release of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas. An improved process was recently reported by Zheng et al.[106] As shown in Figure 6, the cooperative structural transformations that were induced by the reversible photochromic reaction upon UV and visible-light irradiation modulated the gas sorption properties for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas. A sophisticated system demonstrating reversible control of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation was developed by Zhou et al. by integrating the photochromic switch (DTE derivative) and photosensitizer (porphyrin derivative) in a single MOF. Subsequently, they used the MOF as a heterogeneous catalyst for controllable photooxidation of 1,5-dihydroxynaphthalene.[107]
3.2. Functionalization of MOFs by Photochemical Reactions on Metal Clusters and Guest Molecules
Unlike the variety of photochemical reactions that occur on organic linkers, photochemical functionalization of MOF metal clusters is rare, and metal clusters assisted by organic linker photo­sensitizers are generally utilized for photocatalysis. Due to the fast electron–hole recombination of metal clusters, they generate highly reactive species with very short lifetimes, i.e., photochemical reaction intermediates, after photoirradiation. Photogenerated metal cluster reactive species with a long lifetime were reported by Dan-Hardi et al.[108] In 2009, they presented a synthesis and structure of a classic MOF (MIL-125) with photoactive titanium oxo-hydroxo clusters. In the presence of alcohols as sacrificial electron donors, photoirradiation led to the reduction of Ti(IV) into Ti(III) sites in the titanium oxo-clusters of MIL-125. In addition, under an inert atmosphere, a long lifetime Ti(III)- Ti(IV) mixed valence cluster can be obtained and has potential applications in photonic devices and sensors.
There have been a few reports on photochemical reactions that have excluded the photocatalytic reactions in the pores of MOFs. A novel application for the photoactivation of guest compounds in the pores of MOFs was reported by Vos and co-workers. After absorbing photoactive silver nitrate in the pores of MOFs, they prepared 2D quick-response codes or 3D metallic features on MOF single crystals by generating silver nanoparticles via the laser-induced photochemical reduction of silver nitrate.[109] In
2014, Chen et al. developed a europium MOF with photo­active methyl viologen cations $(\mathrm{MV}^{2+})$ . The long-lived $\mathrm{MV^{\bullet+}}$ radicals were generated via photo/thermo-induced electron transfer and ET process.[110] A similar strategy was also applied by Xing et al. to generate $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpy})^{\bullet+}$ radicals with a lifetime of more than 2 months, and the long lifetime was probably due to confining the reactive radicals in the nanospace of the MOF channels.[111] The controllable generation and release of biomolecules have potential applications in biological chemistry. In 2016, Carmona et al. reported a photoinduced release of CO from $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{tacn})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$ cations incorporated in a MOF.[112] Zhang et al. encapsulated coumarin in the channels of Zn-MOF-74.[113] The photochemical reactions of the guest molecules induced coumarin dimerization in the nanospaces and resulted in an appreciable impact on the sorption capacity for $\mathrm{U}^{\mathrm{VI}}$ ions.
## 4. Functionalization of MOFs for Photoluminescent Tuning and Sensing
MOFs naturally have two distinct types of luminescent centers, viz. conjugated organic ligands and/or metal cations or clusters.[114,115] In some cases, the guest molecules within the porous cavities may also contribute to the emission of the framework. The highly regular channel structures and controllable pore sizes of MOFs enable them to serve as rigid/flexible hosts for luminescent guest species (photoactive lanthanide ions and fluorescent dye molecules) to produce guest-induced luminescence.[116] Such attractive characteristics allow us to generate a variety of luminescent MOF materials, which are crucial for photoluminescent tuning, optical devices and barcodes. In addition, their permanent porosity and luminescent output features make MOFs excellent sensing materials. Herein, functionalized MOFs (especially lanthanide-functionalized MOFs) for photophysical applications are introduced, and the general, pure MOFs are not included because they have been previously reviewed.[113–117]
## 4.1. Functionalization of MOFs for Multi-Color Photoluminescence and White-Light Integration
Interest in multi-color-emitting materials, especially white light-emitting materials, stems from their wide applications in panel displays, room lighting and other optical devices.[117] The collaborative properties of the multiple luminescent centers in MOFs are particularly useful for developing multi-color and white-light-emitting materials. Common approaches to realize white-light integration in MOFs include: (a) monochromatic emitters covering the entire visible spectrum, (b) dichromatic emitters blending blue and yellow or orange light, and (c) trichromatic emitters combining three primary color (red, green, and blue) components.[118] In addition, the emission colors of luminescent MOFs can be readily regulated and integrated by modulating both chemical factors (such as the $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ type and concentration) and physical parameters, such as the excitation wavelength. Thus far, tremendous efforts have been dedicated to the synthesis of MOFs with tunable luminescence or white light.[118–128] Ln-based, luminescent MOFs are the most wellcovered MOFs with tunable luminescence properties in the literature.
Sava et al. reported white-light from a single component emitter in an indium-based MOF $\mathrm{(In(BTB)}_{2/3}\mathrm{(OA)(DEF)}_{3/2}$ SMOF-1) featuring an inherent broadband direct white emission with a very high correlated color temperature (CCT) (21642–33290 K).[123] A $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ component $(10\%)$ was incorporated to obtain better color rendering index (CRI) and CCT values than those of SMOF-1.
The organic ligands in MOFs act as building blocks to construct the framework and are also used as blue color sources and sensitizers for $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions. By combining the blue emissions of the ligands with orange luminescent $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ or red-emitting $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , white-light integration based a dual-emitting pathway can be achieved. Zhang et al. synthesized new Ln-MOFs, $[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{dpdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{IP})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{n}.$ , and realized white-light emission in the
Sm-MOF and Eu-Gd-MOF through the dual emissions of the ligand and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ .[124] Yan’s group also demonstrated a dual-emitting strategy to obtain tunable luminescence and white light in $\mathrm{Ag^{+}/S m^{3+}}\ @\mathrm{MIL^{-1}}21$ .[125] Upon excitation at $320~\mathrm{nm}$ , the characteristic emissions of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , which has the strongest peak at $603\ \mathrm{nm}$ , produced an orange color output (Figure 7). Under excitation at 340 and $350\mathrm{nm}$ , its luminescence spectra featured two types of emissions, i.e., $\mathrm{sm}^{3+\prime}\mathrm{s}$ sharp emissions and the ligand-centered broad emission, leading to the overall whitelight emission. Zhou et al. reported a new class of nano-MOFs with tunable and white-light luminescence.[126] By controlled doping of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ into Al-MIL-53-COOH, red, green and yellow luminescence colors were emitted by the resulting products, respectively. Notably, at a certain excitation wavelength $(350~\mathrm{nm})$ or $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ concentration $(10^{-7}\ m o l\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})$ , a white-light luminescence is displayed due to the synergetic contributions from $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and the ligand dual emissions.
A more commonly adopted approach to achieve white-light emission is to blend the blue, green and red light-emitting components in a material. For example, Tang et al. in situ doped $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions into Tb-BTPCA $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTPCA}\ =\ 1,1^{\prime}1^{\prime\prime}.$ -(benzene-1,3,5-triyl)tripiperidine-4-carboxylic acid) to produce $\mathrm{Eu}_{z}\mathrm{Tb}_{1-z}$ -BTPCA. With careful optimization of the molar ratio of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , the $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.005}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.995}$ -BTPCA generated three primary color emissions at 450 (blue), 545 (green), $618~\mathrm{nm}$ (red), and nearly pure white light with a CIE coordinate of (0.3264, 0.3308) was realized.[127] Very recently, Huang et al. utilized an efficient blue-emissive $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ MOF to accommodate redemissive $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ and/or green-emitting $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ cations, resulting in a $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ -encapsulated MOF with a tunable emission color, including red, green, and nearly pure white light.[128]
## 4.2. Functionalization of MOFs for Photoluminescent LED Devices and Barcodes
Due to their tunable optical properties, MOFs are excellent candidates for light-emitting devices and barcodes. Thus far, a variety of luminescent MOFs with tunable emissions have been designed and investigated for light-emitting devices[129–134] and barcodes.[135–137]
Currently, there are two main approaches for fabricating MOF-based WLEDs.[129,130] One approach is to combine a blueLED chip with yellow-emitting phosphors (blue WLEDs). To obtain highly efficient blue-excitable yellow phosphors, Li’s group used a highly emissive ligand (1,1,2,2-tetrakis(4-(pyridin4-yl)phenyl)-ethane $=\mathrm{tppe})$ and a bandgap regulating co-ligand (benzene-1,3,5-tricar-boxylate $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ btc) to construct a zinc-based MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{6}(\mathrm{btc})_{4}(\mathrm{tppe})_{2}(\mathrm{DMA})_{2}];$ and the  highest occupied molecular orbit (HOMO)–lowest unoccupied molecular orbit (LUMO) energy gap was tuned to attain an intrinsic yellow light with an emission maximum at ${\approx}540\$ nm upon excitation with a blue light.[130] A PC-WLED device was assembled by coating the yellow-emitting MOF sample on the surface of a $5\ \mathrm{mm}\ 455{-}460\ \mathrm{nm}$ blue-LED bulb, and the device generated a bright white light when a $3\mathrm{~V~}$ bias was applied. Another method to assemble WLEDs is to coat white phosphors on an ultraviolet LED (UV WLEDs). Sun et al. illustrated a judicious design strategy to achieve efficient, tunable white-light emission by encapsulating a yellow-emitting iridium complex $([\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{Hppy}}=2$ -phenylpyridine) in the cavity of a mesoporous blue-emitting anionic MOF $([(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]_{15}[(\mathrm{Cd}_{2}$ $\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{TATPT})_{4}]\cdot12\mathrm{DMF}\cdot18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{TATPT}}&{{}=}&{2,4,6\mathrm{-tris}(2}\end{array}$ ,5- dicarboxylphenylamino)-1,3,5-triazine).[131] WLED assemblies with superior performances were readily fabricated using the white phosphor and a commercially available UV LED or UV InGaAsN LED chip $(370~\mathrm{nm})$ . Qian’s group developed a warmwhite LED with a good color quality based on a dye-encapsulated MOF phosphor (ZJU-28⊃DSM/AF).[132] The blue color from the anionic MOF ZJU-28 was compensated by the red emission from the cationic dye 4-( $\dot{p}$ -dimethylaminostyryl)-1-methylpyridinium (DSM) and green luminescence from the dye acriflavine (AF), and the output color depended on the contributions of the three components. The sample doped with $0.02\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ DSM and $0.06~\mathrm{wt\%}$ AF exhibited a broadband white emission with the ideal CIE coordinates of (0.34, 0.32), a high CRI value of 91, and a moderate CCT value of 5327 K, which are beneficial for fabricating a warm-white LED device by coating the curved surface of a commercial $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ UV LED chip. Lu et al. presented a novel strategy for creating a near-UV white LED based on the lanthanide-functionalized MOF-based polymer.[133] A yellow-emitting MOF-253 was encapsulated with a red-emitting Eu complex $\left(\mathrm{Eu}_{x}\mathrm{-TTA}\right)$ and was subsequently introduced into an EMA precursor followed by polymerization to form a transparent MOF-based polymer (MOF-PEMA-X). Finally, the MOFbased polymer was assembled on a near-UV GaN chip to realize a near-UV $(395~\mathrm{nm})$ white LED. By altering the X value from 10 to 3.5, the warm white light of the MOF-PEMA-10-based LED with CIE coordinates (0.39, 0.38) changed to a natural white light with CIE coordinates of (0.33, 0.34) in the MOF-PEMA-3.5-based LED. Later, a UV-WLED was fabricated by combining $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -doped UiO-67-bpydc nanophosphors with a UV LED chip,[134] and the emission colors can be finely tuned from the red region to the white region by varying the excitation wavelength (Figure 8a,b). The corresponding LED device generated a bright white light (Figure 8c) with CIE coordinates of (0.35, 0.33), a CCT value of $4914\mathrm{K}$ and a CRI value of 75.
Luminescent-barcoded materials are attractive because of their multiplexed bioanalytical assays and anti-counterfeiting applications. The first luminescent-barcoded system based on MOFs was developed by White and co-workers.[135] They designed a near-IR-emitting $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Er}}_{x}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Yb}}_{1-x}$ -PVDC-1 ( $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=0.32$ , 0.58,
0.70, and 0.81) that simultaneously emitted independent NIR signals from both $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ $(1530~\mathrm{nm})$ and ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ $(980~\mathrm{nm})$ under excitation at either 370 or $470\mathrm{nm}$ . The number and diversity of the barcodes can be increased by incorporating additional lanthanides $(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+})$ , e.g., $\mathrm{Nd}_{0.09}\mathrm{Er}_{0.55}\mathrm{Yb}_{0.36}$ -PVDC-1, which displays a more complicated barcode signal that is composed of three $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ NIR emissions. Inspired by this work, Lu et al. employed a similar encoding strategy to create a new luminescent nanoscale barcode system by incorporating multiple visibleemitting lanthanides $(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+},\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ) into the pores of nano-MOF-253,[136] which was successfully applied to create a functional ionic liquid and ITO glass-supported thin film. Later, Zhou et al. presented a different encoding strategy to construct a multiplexed MOF-based barcode platform for the same purpose,[137] and this strategy relied on a luminescent re-absorption process to obtain numerous different emission intensities in $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}{\ @\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}100(\mathrm{In})$ films, which act as optical codes. As shown in Figure 9a, by loading different amounts of fluorescein isothiocyanate (FL) into the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}100(\mathrm{In})$ films, the emission of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ at $488\mathrm{nm}$ was selectively re-absorbed to various degrees by the FL, and the emission at $544~\mathrm{nm}$ was not re-absorbed by FL and could be used as a ratiometric reference signal $\left(I_{\mathrm{ref}}\right)$ . Similarly, loading methylene blue (MB) into $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}100(\mathrm{In})$ led to a decrease in the emission of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ at $615~\mathrm{nm}$ , and the unfiltered emission at $700\ \mathrm{nm}$ was the reference (Figure 9b, left). The ratiometric optical signals ( $\dot{I}_{\mathrm{code}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{ref}})$ can be translated into color-related codes (Figure 9c,d, left). The emissions at 488 $(I_{\mathrm{code1}})$ and $615\ \mathrm{nm}\ (I_{\mathrm{code}2})$ were filtered by the FL and MB dyes, respectively (Figure 9a, right). When the loading concentrations of both the FL and MB dyes were set at four different levels, a total of 16 ratiometric codes $\left(I_{\mathrm{code1}}/I_{\mathrm{ref}}\right)$ and $I_{\mathrm{code}2}/I_{\mathrm{ref}})$ were obtained (Figure 9b, right).
## 4.3. Functionalization of MOFs for Photoluminescent Chemical Sensors
The collaborative functionalities of the intrinsic porosity and luminescent properties of MOFs make them very promising as a new type of sensory material.[138–142] The porous, crystalline MOF architecture provides several advantages.[137–141] Most notably, the tunable pores and functional groups within the framework, such as Lewis acidic or basic sites on the ligand, and/or open metal sites, facilitate molecular sieving and preferential adsorption of analytes, resulting in high selectivity in MOFs. In addition, the high porosity and host–guest interaction can preconcentrate the guest molecules, which can enhance the sensing sensitivity. Moreover, compared to amorphous materials, the highly ordered crystalline nature of MOFs allows for precise structural determinations and specific knowledge of structural changes that occur during the detection of an analyte and the detail sensing mechanism. To date, a wide range of luminescent MOFs, particularly lanthanide-based MOFs, have been realized and reported as sensors for ions,[143–167] small molecules,[168–177] gas/vapors,[178–190] temperature,[191–194] pH values,[195,196] and moisture.[197]
## 4.3.1. Ion Sensors
Luminescence-based detection of ions with a MOF sensor was first reported by Liu et al. in 2004,[144] and since then, the field has rapidly expanded. Similar examples for sensing ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ were also demonstrated by Yan’s group.[145] Instead of an in situ synthesis of Ln-MOFs, Hao et al. utilized a PSM approach to obtain a lanthanide-doped MOF $\left(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot121\right)$ ). The waterstable $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot121$ showed excellent selectivity for ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ and only ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ drastically enhanced the luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and induced a visible color change from colorless to bright red under UV light. $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}@\mathrm{MLL}\cdot121$ was also utilized to detect ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ in aqueous solutions, and it showed a higher selectivity and sensitivity than $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot121$ .[125] The enhanced luminescence responses of both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot121$ and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}(\underline{{{a}}})\mathrm{ML}.$ - 121 for ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ resulted from the ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ -induced increased antenna efficiency.
Iron species, present in both the $+2$ and $+3$ oxidation states, are essential for virtually all organisms. Therefore, qualitative and quantitative detection of iron is of great importance for human health. Using the free pyridyl nitrogens within MOF253 as sensing sites, Lu et al. developed MOF-253 as a highly sensitive nanoprobe for $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in aqueous solutions and living cells.[146] Binding of the pyridyl nitrogen with $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ caused an effective PET process from MOF-253 to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , which led to quenching of the luminescence of MOF-253. Zhou et al. also fabricated a highly luminescent $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -doped MIL-53-COOH (Al) nanocrystal $\left(\operatorname{Eu}^{3+}({\widehat{\underline{{a}}}}1\right)$ and used it as a highly selective and sensitive probe to detect $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in aqueous solutions (Figure 10).[147] The quenching effect of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ on the emission of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@1$ was accomplished through the ion exchange of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with the framework $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ ions, which suppressed the ET from the ligand to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ . A layer-like MOF $\mathrm{(Ga_{2}(O H)_{4}(C_{9}O_{6}H_{4}))}$ loaded with $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ was reported by Yan’s group and realized the simultaneous detection of both $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ .[148] In this case, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions quenched the emissions of both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and the MOF, and the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions only quenched the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ luminescence.
In addition, other toxic cations (such as $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ,[149] $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ,[150,151] and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+[152]}\big)$ and metal ions in living organisms, such as $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ,[153] $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ,[154] ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ ,[155] $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ ,[156] and $\mathbb{A}^{1^{3+}}$ ,[119,157] have also been detected by luminescent MOFs.
Utilizing luminescent MOFs as a platform for sensing anions[158–168] has been investigated in the past decade. To detect $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ , Chen et al. reported the luminescent MOF-76 to selectively sense $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ based on a luminescence enhancement effect,[158] and two other luminescent Ln-MOFs sensors $\left(\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{BPDC})(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right)_{n}$ and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{btec})\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ ， which were reported by Zhou et al.[159] and Hao et al.,[120] showed quenching responses to $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ . Shi et al. demonstrated that two cationic MOFs, $\mathrm{[Ln}_{2}\mathrm{Zn(L)}_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}](\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{n}$ $(\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}$ , Tb; $\mathrm{L}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylate- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -dipyridine anion), were highly sensitive and selective, qualitative, fast and reversible turn-off luminescent probes for detecting $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ in aqueous solutions, and the formation of ${\mathrm{I}}_{3}^{-}$ in the channels was the reason for the sensing behaviors.[160] Liu et al. fabricated a luminescent Eu-HBPTC-based MOF thin film, which exhibited a specific luminescence quenching response for carbonate.[161] Sun et al. also explored a ratiometric luminescent sensor $(\mathrm{Eu/Pt-MOF})$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}$ that was based on a remarkable enhancement in the emission intensity ratio of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ at $614~\mathrm{nm}$ to ligand centered at $560~\mathrm{nm}$ .[162] $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ can cause serious pollution in aquatic ecosystems as a pollutant anion. $\mathtt{X u}$ et al. demonstrated a waterstable Eu-MOF, $\{\mathrm{[Eu_{1.5}(B T B)_{1.5}(H_{2}O)]\cdot3D M F}\}_{n},$ sensor that can sensitively, selectively, and recurrently detect the pollutant $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ via its drastic quenching effect on the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ emission.[163] Contrary to the previously reported phenomenon, Yang et al. realized specific recognition of phosphate through a coordination-induced fluorescence enhancement effect using the high affinity of phosphate for the $\mathsf{Z r{-}O}$ nodes in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .[164] Mandal’s group recently developed a turn-on $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ chemical sensor, $\mathrm{[Tb(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)(BTB)]}$ , by a PSM metal node exchange of $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ in $\mathrm{[H_{2}N(C H_{3})_{2}][B a(H_{2}O)(B T B)]}$ with $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ .[165] Other attractive anionic sensing targets are toxic chromium (VI) anion species, and they have been detected by many luminescent MOF sensors based on a quenching mechanism.[166,167]
## 4.3.2. Small-Organic-Molecule Sensors
Small organic molecules, especially volatile organic compounds (VOCs), are toxic pollutants, and their recognition by luminescent MOFs has been widely investigated over the past decade. DMF and acetone are common solvents and have been detected by many luminescent MOF sensors.[168–170] In addition, other toxic organic solvents, such as cyclohexane, 1,4-dioxane, and nitromethane, which are widely employed in industrial applications, have also been detected by luminescent MOF sensors. A novel MOF, $\left\{[\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{edc})(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}]\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right\}_{n:n}$ , that was created by Hou et al. was the first luminescent sensor to recognize cyclohexane and nitrobenzene through a luminescence enhancement and quenching effect, respectively.[171] Zhou et al. explored a self-calibrating color-gradient luminescent sensor for mixed 1,4-dioxane/glycol based on a bimetallic Ln-MOF material, $[\mathrm{Eu}_{2x}\mathrm{Tb}_{2(1-x)}(\mathrm{FDA})_{3}],$ .[172] The two emissive centers of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ at $616~\mathrm{nm}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaTb^{3+}}$ at $546~\mathrm{nm}$ in $[\mathrm{Eu}_{0.5}\mathrm{Tb}_{1.5}(\mathrm{FDA})_{3}]$ exhibited opposite volume ratio-dependent luminescence behaviors for the 1,4-dioxane/glycol mixture, which were accompanied by visible luminescence color changes, providing a self-calibrating color-gradient sensing mechanism. Yan et al. synthesized two Ln-MOFs, $\{\mathrm{[Ln(L)(BPDC)_{1/2}(N O_{3})]\cdot H_{3}O}\}_{n}$ $\mathrm{{Ln}=\ E u,T b}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}=$ 2,5-di(pyridin-4-yl)terephthalic acid), and utilized them to detect nitromethane.[173]
The luminescent MOF sensor mentioned above generally detects specific organic molecules from among several mole­cules, but the sensor cannot simultaneously differentiate multi­ple organic molecules. A clever molecular decoding strategy was presented by Kitagawa and co-workers,[174] and since then, some luminescent MOF sensors have been designed and reported for decoding VOCs. By the self-assembly of Rhodamine B into a porous CZJ-3 MOF, Dong et al. exploited a luminescent Rho $@$ CZJ-3 platform to probe different VOCs by modulating the ET efficiency between the emissions of the MOF host and dye guest.[175] Rho $@$ CZJ-3 exhibited an excellent fingerprint correlation between the VOCs and the emission peak-height ratio of the ligand to the dye, resulting in clearly differentiable and unique readouts for different VOCs. Zhang et al. designed a 2D layer MOF named NUS-1, and its activated analogue, NUS-1a, exhibited a responsive turn-on fluorescence to various VOCs that was accompanied by emission peak shifts $(\lambda_{\mathrm{fl}})$ .[176] Recently, Zhou et al. established a responsive and reusable MOF nanocomposite $\mathrm{[Eu^{3+}}@$ bpy-UiO) with two emitters, the bpy ligand and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , to decode aromatic VOCs with similar structures and physical properties, even homologues and isomers.[177] Their emission intensity ratio $(I_{\mathrm{L}}/I_{\mathrm{Eu}})$ had a one-toone correspondence to the encapsulated VOCs (Figure 11a,b). In addition, the luminescence quantum yield $(\Phi)$ of $\mathbb{E}\mathrm{u}^{3+}@\mathsf{b}\mathsf{p}\mathsf{y}-$ UiO was also responsive to the VOCs. Therefore, the identities of the analyzed VOCs could be succinctly decoded into a corresponding 2D readout $(\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{L}}}/\boldsymbol{I_{\mathrm{Eu}}},\boldsymbol{\Phi})$ , as displayed in Figure 11.
## 4.3.3. Gas/Vapor Sensors
In the past few years, some luminescent MOFs have also been explored for sensing gas-phase analytes, including common gases and vapors of solids/liquids.[142,178–190]
Formaldehyde, which is a typical indoor pollutant, has been classified as a human carcinogen by IARC.[186] In addition to naked-eye detection, exposure to a formaldehyde vapor at a concentration as low as 0.0016 ppm over $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ caused a detectable increase in the emission of the sensor. To probe indoor formaldehyde with a high selectivity and sensitivity, Hao et al. designed a dual-emissive $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ -Eu(III)-functionalized MOF nanoprobe $(\mathrm{Ag^{+}/E u^{3+}}@\mathrm{UiO.}66\ –(\mathrm{COOH})_{2})$ .[180] The implantation of the ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ ions allowed the material to realize the dual-luminescence of both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and the organic ligands via disturbing the ET from the ligand to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ (LMET), and the ions also served as the reactive sites for formaldehyde. The reaction between the ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ ions and the formaldehyde vapors weakened the ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ ion inhibition of the LMET process, which induced an increase in the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ luminescence and a decrease in the ligand-based emission (Figure 12). The opposite luminescence responses of the two emitters to the formaldehyde vapor endowed the probe with a self-calibrating sensing ability for indoor formaldehyde. The ratiometric nanoprobe also demonstrated a high sensitivity with a detection limit of $51\ \mathrm{ppb}$ and a fast response $({\approx}6\ \mathrm{min})$ . Recently, $\mathrm{\DeltaXu}$ et al. reported the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -functionalized $Z{\mathrm{nO}}\ @\operatorname{UiO}-$ 67, which was capable of detecting aldehyde vapors (formaldehyde, acetaldehyde and acraldehyde) in vehicles.[181] Aldehyde vapors can react with the active oxygen species on the surface of the $\mathsf{Z n O}$ nanoparticles in the material, which is accompanied by a charge release, and the released charge further transfers to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , resulting in a luminescence enhancement of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and selective recognition of aldehyde vapors.
Oxygen gas has attracted attention because of its practical significance in environments. In 2011, An et al. reported a recyclable $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensor based on a NIR-luminescent ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}.$ exchanged bio-MOF-1.[182] Upon exposure to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gas, the NIR luminescence signal at $980~\mathrm{nm}$ of $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}@\mathrm{bio}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{MOF}{\mathrm{-}}1$ decreased by $\approx40\%$ within $5~\mathrm{min}$ . Two years later, the oxygen sensing capabilities of two $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ postfunctionalized luminescent MOF films on ITO glass substrates, $\mathrm{CPM}{\cdot}5{\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and MIL $.^{-100}(\mathrm{In}){\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , were also demonstrated by Dou et al.[183] The two films exhibited reversible oxygen quenching and nitrogen recovering properties with alternating cycles of 1 atm of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . The $\scriptstyle\lambda\operatorname{IL-100}(\operatorname{In})\supset\operatorname{Tb}^{3+}$ film showed a higher oxygen sensitivity $\left(K_{\mathrm{sv}}=7.59\right)$ and shorter response/recovery time $(6~\mathrm{{s}}/53~\mathrm{{s})}$ than that of CPM- ${5}\mathrm{{=}}\mathrm{{T}}{\mathsf{b}}^{3+}$ , which was ascribed to the much higher ET efficiency in MI $\mathrm{L}{\cdot}100(\mathrm{In}){\supset}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . Considering the higher $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ permeability and more flexible properties of soft films compared with those of rigid membranes, Xu et al. recently fabricated a soft membrane oxygen sensor (NMOFs $@0–\mathrm{PP}$ , Figure 13a) by in situ growth of SUMOF6-Eu on flexible, nonwoven polypropylene (PP) surfaces and coated it with a water-resistant poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PDMS) layer.[184] The coated NMOFs $@$ O-PP soft films with a moisture stability showed a high luminescence quenching efficiency of $89.9\%$ with 1 atm $\mathrm{O}_{2}.$ , a low detection limit $(0.45\%)$ , fast response and good reversibility (Figure 13b).
In addition, other gases or vapors, such as $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , NO, HCl, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , and DMF, have been detected by luminescent MOFs. Using a postsynthetic linker exchange route, Nickerl et al. integrated the oxidation-sensitive dihydro-1,2,4,5-tetrazine-3,6-dicarboxylate into the UiO-66 framework to create the colorimetric chemosensor, UiO-66(dhtz), to detect oxidizing agents, such as nitrous gases.[185] Zhao et al. reported an in situ self-assembled $\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}}$ -MOF-based mixed matrix membrane, which can act as a naked-eye and luminescent sensor for gaseous HCl with a high sensitivity and selectivity.[186] Wang et al. designed a recyclable on–off–on luminescent Eu-MOF sensor for HCl and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ vapors.[187] A luminescent film based on a $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -functionalized nano-MOF $(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{OH})\mathrm{bg}$ ydc) was reported by Hao et al. and showed a selective quenching response to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ vapors among various indoor air pollutants with an excellent sensitivity (detection limit of $2.4~\mathrm{ppm})$ , fast response $(\approx30\ \mathrm{s})$ , and quick regeneration with no cost.[188] Li et al. synthesized a new luminescent MOF $\mathrm{([Eu_{2}L(H_{2}O)_{4}]\cdot3D M F}$ $\mathrm{[L}=2^{\prime},5^{\prime}$ -bis(methoxymethyl)- $[1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $4,4^{\prime\prime}$ -dicarboxylate) that showed a selective turn-on luminescence response to DMF vapor.[189]
(Figure 14a,b). A good relationship was observed between the intensity ratio $(I_{\mathrm{Tb},545\mathrm{nm}}/I_{\mathrm{Eu},613\mathrm{nm}})$ and the temperature (Figure 14c), and the thermal sensitivity was $4.97\%^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{-1}$ , indicating that this mixed-lanthanide MOF fabricated by PSM was a highly sensitive temperature sensor. In addition, a conceptually different approach for designing ratiometric luminescent thermometers was reported by Cui et al.[191] and incorporated the organic dye perylene into an Eu-MOF (ZJU-88). Using the opposite temperature-dependent properties of the dual emissions from organic dyes and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions, ratiometric temperature sensing in the physiological temperature range $(20{-}80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was realized, and the unique ET process from the perylene molecules to the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions led to a high sensitivity of $1.28\%^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{-1}$ .[194]
## 4.3.4. Temperature Sensors
Luminescent MOFs are promising platforms for designing temperature sensors because the multiple luminescence centers and ET processes within MOF materials are susceptible to temperature.
Qian and co-workers have reported a number of studies in this domain[190,191] and recently published a review.[192] They pioneered a mixed-lanthanide MOF approach to develop ratiometric luminescent thermometers.[190] By doping $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ into an isostructural $\mathrm{Tb^{3+}{\cdot}M O F}$ , mixed-lanthanide MOFs $(\mathrm{Eu}_{x}\mathrm{Tb}_{1-x^{-}}$ DMBDC, DMBDC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}2,5$ -dimethoxy-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) with both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ emissions were obtained. The two emissions in $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.0069}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.9931}$ -DMBDC exhibited opposite responses to the temperature, and their intensity ratio $({I_{\mathrm{Tb}}}/{I_{\mathrm{Eu}}})$ linearly correlated with the temperature in the range of 50 to $200~\mathrm{{K}}$ . The unique response behavior was attributed to an enhanced ET from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ with the increasing temperature. To circumvent the challenges of the rational design and synthesis of nanoscale mixed-lanthanide MOFs, Zhou et al. presented a straightforward postsynthetic strategy to fabricate ratiometric luminescent nano-thermometers.[193] Both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions were simultaneously encapsulated in the pores of the $\mathrm{In(OH)}$ (bpydc) nano-MOF via a binding interaction with the accessible $2^{\prime}2$ -bipyridyl sites within the framework. As the temperature increased from 10 to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ at $545~\mathrm{nm}$ dramatically decreased, and the emission intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ at $613\ \mathrm{nm}$ slightly increased
## 4.3.5. Biological Monitoring and Biomarker Sensors
To our knowledge, most of the reported luminescent MOFbased sensors for toxic substances are generally based on external/environmental monitoring. As a supplement to environmental monitoring, biological monitoring, which allows for accurate quantification of the pollutant that is absorbed by humans instead of the external exposure levels estimated by environmental monitoring, plays a significant role in health risk assessment. Biomarkers are generally used as effective indicators for biological monitoring.[198] Yan’s group performed pioneering work on the determination of biomarkers for biomonitoring human exposure to pollutants (such as toluene, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), vinyl chloride, polystyrene, etc.) by luminescent MOF sensors.[199–202]
Urinary hippuric acid (HA) is a biological indicator of human exposure to toxic toluene. To estimate exposure to toluene, the urinary HA is typically determined because it can better reflect the actual amount of toluene inhaled by individuals. Hao et al. reported the first use of a water-stable, $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -functionalized MOF to detect HA (Figure 15a),[199] and the MOF had a remarkable, selective reduction in the luminescence $(84\%)$ for HA among the various urine ingredients, which was accompanied by a distinguishable emission color change from bright red to dark red (Figure 15b). Additionally, the quenching response was linear for the logarithm of the HA concentration over the range from 0.05 to $8~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ (Figure 15c), and the LOD was $9~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ More importantly, this probe was successfully applied for the determination of HA in human urine, making it promising for the point-of-care diagnosis of diseases associated with occupational toluene exposure.
PAHs are carcinogenic and ubiquitous environmental contaminants. The measurement of urinary 1-HP, a marker of the PAH total internal dose, is an important method for assessing PAH exposure since it takes into account all the absorption routes of exogenous PAHs. To detect 1-HP, a luminescent Eu-doped MOF $(\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{btec})\cdot0.5\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ sensor based on the LMET and FRET process (Figure 16) was designed.[201] The ligand-centered emission of the MOF had a large spectral overlap with the absorbance spectrum of 1-HP, which resulted in an efficient FRET from the ligand to 1-HP. The occurrence of the FRET blocked the ligand-to-Eu ET and caused luminescence quenching of the Eu-doped MOF to realize the sensing of 1-HP with an outstanding performance, including an excellent anti-interference ability, high sensitivity with a detection limit as low as $0.69\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , a short response time of ${\approx}1$ min and good recyclability (Figure 16b). The good sensing performance of the probe enabled it to monitor 1-HP in real human urine specimens. Moreover, a portable 1-HP urine test paper was also developed to evaluate the PAH intoxication degree by the naked eye (Figure 16c).
To assess and prevent the occupational risks for workers exposed to styrene, the most frequently used biological monitoring method is to determine the concentration of the biomarker phenylglycolic acid (PGA) in urine or serum. Lian et al. reported an unprecedented turn-on fluorescence switch based on a $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -loaded Sc-MOF for the detection of ppb level urinary and ppm level serous PGA with an appealing sensing performance.[202] Moreover, the on-site detection of PGA was realized by a paper-based probe fabricated via coating the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ - loaded Sc-MOF onto the filter paper.
## 5. Conclusions and Perspectives
Here, recent research progress on functionalized MOFs for photoactive materials is summarized. For photochemical applications, two topics are involved, photocatalytic solar fuel production and photochemical response. The ultrahigh porosity and tailorable functionality of MOFs have resulted in impressive developments related to photocatalytic solar fuel production, and further advancements in the photocatalytic efficiency are expected. The hierarchical assembly of multicomponent MOF materials containing site-isolated, redox-active metal nodes, photoactive antenna linkers, and extra framework cocatalysts will provide a synergistic biomimetic matrix for photocatalysis. The utilization of photochemical reactions facilitates the intuitive observance of the transformation process in an
SCSC manner and elucidates the reaction mechanisms. The functionalization of MOFs will result in multi-functionalities such as sensing, switching and gas storage by taking advantage of photochemical reactions. More efforts should be devoted to the synthesis and stabilization of highly reactive or reaction intermediate species in solid MOF crystal lattices via photochemical reactions. For photophysical applications of functionalized MOFs, luminescent tuning, device barcodes and chemical sensing were outlined. In this regard, much work is still needed to fabricate MOF-based thin films and devices, which will accelerate the use of these multifunctional materials in real-world applications. The development of MOF-based biomedical devices for the sensitive detection of biomolecules still requires further investigation for the early diagnosis of diseases. With extensive research and continuing efforts in the design and functionalization of MOFs, MOFs will be used for photoactive applications in the near future.
